A||||9||2M4 Dale||The Collectable Fiero?||||1
Z||000000||2M4 Dale||10-15-1999||04:05 AM||||Does anyone see Fieros becoming the GTOs or 442s of the future? You know high dollar concourse originals and such. Seems the diminutive Corvair is enjoying a popularity surge. Will the aftermarket parts suppliers start making resto parts? Like The Paddock and Year One does for old Goats and Chevelles. Think about it...Right now Fiero's are selling for $1000 to $4000 on average. We could be sitting on a gold mine. In 1981 I bought a 69 Judge for $3500. Right now (if its still in good condition) its probably worth $20,000.||||reg||1
Z||000001||RossT||10-15-1999||05:25 AM||||There is a good article about the collectibility of fieros at:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.xnet.com/~paulv/" TARGET=_blank>www.xnet.com/~paulv/</A> <P>click on  fiero values, then on collector car<P>Back in 88, alot of people bought 88gt's and threw them into storage with delivery<BR>mileage on the odometer. Even back then<BR>they were thinking about the collectibility of<BR>the fiero.  Many of these cars have come up for sale recently with prices ranging from<BR>$12,000 to $25,000. They normally sell<BR>very fast. The guy I bought my 88gt (475miles) rectenly from, had numerous calls from  europe inquiring about it!!!<P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan||10-16-1999||06:42 AM|||| Absolutely. Years ago I should have collected 68 cameros,especially when many were heading to the boneyard.Right now the 4 cylinders are depriciated as much as they will, the 6 is lagging behind a bit.I have talked to several collectors/restorers who love these cars, even if they don't have one now.Problem is right now that so many are headed to the boneyard that it makes the price of them bottom out.This "used" cycle used to happen in the older cars at 8 years,but the better built cars of the eightys extended this to about 15 years.There are many great Fieros with a bad engine or transmission going to the boneyard and the yards won't pay much for them.I suggest you guys run an ad in your local trader,wanted:Fieros for parts/restore.This may get you first choice on many great Fieros.In addition,there are so many parcularities related to Fieros and many shops don't want to work on them without charging double.As more Fieros go to the yard,the price will climb.Supply and demand.Immagine in 10 years,20 years having this unique American 2 seat mid-engine sports car in restored shape in your stable.I now own 4and would like to get another hundred or two. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||10-16-1999||10:35 AM||||I agree with Dan, Fieros are and will become a collectors item, infact the trend has started. One of the things that helps the Fiero go up in price is the fact that people like to know that there is a shop that will work on their Fieros since a lot of dealers won't work on them. I get told this by many of my customers, They say that they were thinking of getting rid of their Fieros but now that they know I'm here they will keep their car and restore it. That's what I've been doing a lot of is restoration and the value goes up.||||reg||10
Z||000004||88 Fiero||10-16-1999||12:34 PM||||The most desired to be a collector Fiero's will the "Indy Fiero's" and the "88 Models...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Boxdin||10-16-1999||01:40 PM||||I knew 10 years ago there would be "collectability status". I could go on for hours, but i never saw an advertising campaign like Pontiac's. They hyped the car like crazy for 3 years, spent 87 muddling, and 88 and beyond in denial. I bought a new Formula in 88, and within a year I recieved monthly offers for an extra 4 or 5 thousand on a trade in from Pontial directly. It's like they wanted to get all the cars off the streets ASAP||||reg||14
Z||000006||terryk||10-16-1999||07:43 PM||||Considering how many were built, I believe they will remain a "dime-a-dozen". There are so many still on the road. Maybe twenty five years from now, they might be worth a bit more, but something will come along and replace it. Most are well into their second and third owners now. <P>Buying a Fiero as an investment is unwise IMHO. Owning one for fun is the best reason.<BR> Way too many were cranked out. Even the 88's are not at all that rare. A bit more rare, but not impossible to find.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||2M4 Dale||10-16-1999||11:45 PM||||I know Pontiac made a bunch of these cars. But only for a few years. Look how long Camaros and Mustangs have been in production. The Fiero also has the bonus of being a very unique American automobile in that it is the only mid engine production car ever made in the States. (I THINK) I'm gonna' restore mine and then get another one for good measure....When I go out to the parking lot (anywhere) I don't have to play witch one is my Camaro. Thanks guys for all your response!||||reg||14
Z||000008||87GT||10-17-1999||09:39 PM||||I have read all over the internet that the fieros to watch for climbing value will be the Indy fieros and 86.5-88 GTs.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||10-17-1999||11:16 PM||||Posted by the Indy and GT owners!<P>Ok, I couldn't resist!||||reg||1
